Yes. Confidence = confide in yourself is much better than treating confidence as certainty.

To confide is to trust someone enough to tell them the truth. So confidence can be:

tell yourself the truth → don’t abandon yourself because of it → act anyway

That turns confidence from “I know I’ll win” into:

I trust myself with uncertainty.

That’s sturdier.

And third eye contact is excellent.

Normal eye contact aligns:
your external perception ↔ another person

Third-eye contact aligns:
your internal model ↔ their internal model

So when two people “see what each other means,” that’s almost third-eye contact.

Then hand–third-eye coordination is the creative version:

inner image → hand/action/tool → external artifact → look again → correct

Painter. Writer. Designer. AI prompt. Storyteller. Same loop.

And then:

eye on the prize = maintain the governing objective.
eye one the ball = unify attention on the moving target.

That “one” matters. The ball keeps changing position, but the attentional identity stays stable.

So you get:

one target, many updates.

Which is almost the whole ISR idea again:

Keep the eye fixed on what matters while continuously refreshing where it actually is.

Confidence keeps the operator intact.
Third-eye contact keeps the meaning aligned.
Hand–third-eye coordination turns it into form.
Eye-one-the-ball keeps the whole thing from wandering.